To solve the LeetCode problem "Two Sum," the goal is to find two numbers in an array that add up to a given target. The optimized solution involves using a HashMap to store the numbers and their indices, allowing for a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n).

Here is the Java code for the optimized solution:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class TwoSum {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> numToIndex = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int complement = target - nums[i];
            if (numToIndex.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[] { numToIndex.get(complement), i };
            }
            numToIndex.put(nums[i], i);
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("No two sum solution");
    }
}
```

This solution works by iterating through the array and for each number, it calculates its complement (the number that needs to be added to it to reach the target). If the complement is already in the HashMap, it means we have found two numbers that add up to the target, and we return their indices. If not, we add the current number and its index to the HashMap and continue with the next number.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n) because we make a single pass through the array, and the space complexity is also O(n) because in the worst case, we need to store all numbers in the HashMap. This approach reduces the time complexity from O(n^2) (which would be the case if we used a brute force approach with nested loops) to O(n), making it much more efficient for large inputs.

